--TEST--
Core\Arr::flattenDeep answers the same leaves at every nesting depth including one far past a recursive walk's stack, and an array written into itself flattens to the snapshot it stored rather than diverging, per spec § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/arr.rs:2219`'s doc gives two reasons for the shape of
// the walk, and no case asks about either. It uses "an explicit stack rather
// than recursion" because "the nesting depth is the caller's data, and a deeply
// nested argument must not be able to reach the host stack's limit"; and it
// keeps no visited set because "an array cannot contain itself — it is a
// copy-on-write value, so `$a[] = $a` stores a copy". The sibling cases
// `arr-flatten-deep-is-flatten-iterated-to-a-fixed-point` and
// `arr-flatten-unwraps-one-level-and-flatten-deep-all-of-them` both work over
// nestings an array literal can write, which is where neither reason bites.

// The same two leaves under a sweep of depths, the last of them 5,000 levels —
// far past what a recursive walk could take a frame at a time, and a depth no
// literal in this file could spell. The answer is invariant: what changes with
// the depth is only how far the walk has to go.
array<int> $depths = [0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 5000];
int $asked = 0;
int $same = 0;
foreach ($depths as int $d) {
    array<mixed> $nested = ["a", "b"];
    int $wrapped = 0;
    while ($wrapped < $d) {
        $nested = [$nested];
        $wrapped = $wrapped + 1;
    }
    $asked = $asked + 1;
    if (Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::flattenDeep($nested)) == "[\"a\",\"b\"]") {
        $same = $same + 1;
    }
}
echo $asked as string, " ", $same as string, "\n";

// What the sweep's rows actually are, so six agreements are not six flat
// arrays: the same construction at depth 3, printed before it is flattened.
array<mixed> $three = ["a", "b"];
int $i = 0;
while ($i < 3) {
    $three = [$three];
    $i = $i + 1;
}
echo Core\Json::encode($three), " ", Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::flattenDeep($three)), "\n";

// The second reason. Writing an array into itself stores the value it had at
// the moment of the write — `rule:types/array-combination`'s copy-on-write array is a value, not a
// handle — so the walk is finite with nothing tracking what it has visited.
// Once, then again over the result, so the nesting the second write stores is
// itself already a nested one.
array<mixed> $a = ["x"];
$a["self"] = $a;
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::flattenDeep($a)), "\n";
$a["again"] = $a;
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::flattenDeep($a)), "\n";

// And the subject that produced those two answers, which is what makes them
// snapshots rather than cycles: `self` holds the one-entry array, and `again`
// holds the two-entry one that already contained it. Neither holds `$a` as it
// stands now.
echo Core\Json::encode($a), "\n";
--EXPECT--
6 6
[[[["a","b"]]]] ["a","b"]
["x","x"]
["x","x","x","x"]
{"0":"x","self":["x"],"again":{"0":"x","self":["x"]}}
